\f4\fs26 The contractors on site had no idea why they were pouring such an enormous amount of concrete into a non-descript hole in the ground. A couple of eye-brows were raised when they were told the composition of the mix, the strangest concoction of additives they had ever seen, mainly lead and carbon-fibre, but to their surprise, apart from needing a bit of sugar to stop it going off too quickly, it seemed to be top quality mud. There was a bit of a problem when a lab roof caved in and a few big bubbles popped at the surface, but this was easily remedied by pouring an extra hundred cubic metres of concrete, a bonanza for the contractors.\
The sickness came within a week or two when a group of concrete contractors fell ill and died suddenly, followed by their families and neighbours. A genetically altered and more virulent version of Ebola, it spread quickly across the biggest city in Ulyssia. Given wings by modern genetics, 
\f5\i Ebola-Papillion
\f4\i0 , spread easily though the air, taking the lives of all it struck down. It became known as the \'93Black Butterfly\'94 as it flitted and landed indiscriminately, randomly striking all but a handful in an entire neighbourhood, while only plucking out an unlucky few elsewhere. \
The common link between the victims, it was surmised, was genetic, but all the best geneticists, virologists and microbiologists had been encased in concrete and were unable to assist. The only locally produced geneticist with the requisite qualifications, Dr Linus Grawling, had managed to get tenure at the University of Memphis after being hounded out of the country for his error ridden attempts at human cloning. The mangled lumps of semi-humanity he had left behind in the PTD were sufficiently gruesome for warrants to be issued, forcing Grawling to flee the jurisdiction. If they wanted his assistance, they would have to drop all the charges and allow him to continue his work unhindered. He had made significant progress with a genome he had stumbled across in Memphis and felt a breakthrough was within reach.\
Dr Grawling was welcomed back with open arms and quickly solved the mystery. The virus was engineered and would only target a specific genetic profile, about 25% to 30% of the population. He just as quickly manufactured a testing regime that would identify persons at risk, although it was not well received, in that it merely identified those who were going to die. Thousands, blessed with the news that they would most certainly die, went on a murderous rampage, determined to take as people with them as time allowed. \
The plague and rioting raged on for years, hunting down anyone without natural immunity. It was reported that the greatest man of their age, Jamison Hardy, had disappeared and was presumed taken by the plague. \
